/-!
# Linear magmas over orders whose coefficient modulus has no rational prime

`Definability/LinearOrders.lean` proves `hc` -- that `1 + c x` never vanishes -- by exhibiting a
reduction `ℤ[θ] → ZMod m` that kills `c`. Such a reduction exists exactly when some prime of
residue degree one divides `c`, and for the two orders here none does: the norm of `c` is `25` and
`9`, carried by primes of degree two.

The fix is to let the residue map land in `F_{p^2} = F_p[φ] / (g)` instead, `g` a quadratic factor
of `q` mod `p` that also divides `c`. `Magma.add_mul_ne_zero_of_hom` is already stated for an
arbitrary target ring, and `AdjoinRoot.nontrivial` supplies the only thing that ring must satisfy,
`1 ≠ 0`; irreducibility of `g` is never needed. The divisions witnessing `g ∣ q` and `g ∣ c` are
exact only modulo `p`, so each `linear_combination` also carries `(p : F) = 0`.

This file is generated by `genordff.py`.
-/

